================================================================================
A  MODEL COMPARISON on the mean per-destination slope (n = 20 residues)
================================================================================
LORO = leave-one-RESIDUE-out cross-validated R^2. In-sample R^2 always rises
with parameters at n=20; LORO is the number that can go NEGATIVE and does.

model                        k       R^2   adj R^2  LORO R^2
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KD                           1    0.7122    0.6962    0.6672
dGtr                         1    0.7903    0.7787    0.7441
VOL                          1    0.2056    0.1615    0.0660
POLARSA                      1    0.5350    0.5091    0.4394
BETA                         1    0.6183    0.5971    0.5524
KD + VOL                     2    0.8839    0.8702    0.8385
dGtr + VOL                   2    0.7988    0.7751    0.7033
KD + dGtr                    2    0.8347    0.8152    0.7890
KD + POLARSA                 2    0.7166    0.6833    0.6103
dGtr + POLARSA               2    0.7959    0.7719    0.7110
KD + dGtr + VOL              3    0.8954    0.8758    0.8325

BEST BY LORO: KD + VOL (LORO R^2 = 0.8385)
VOLUME VERDICT: VOL alone LORO = 0.0660;  adding VOL to KD moves LORO 0.6672 -> 0.8385 (+0.1713)

================================================================================
B  IS THE AXIS ALREADY IN THE INPUT? -- the arithmetic point
================================================================================
The feature vector ends in one_hot(20) = the destination residue, exact and
lossless. A per-residue scalar table T is the matrix product one_hot @ T,
i.e. a rank-1 linear projection of a block fc1 ALREADY receives.
Demonstration: the saturated one-hot model has k=20 free parameters on n=20
points, so it interpolates every table exactly.
   one_hot @ w_KD     reproduces the table exactly, max|err| = 0.0e+00
   one_hot @ w_dGtr   reproduces the table exactly, max|err| = 0.0e+00
   one_hot @ w_VOL    reproduces the table exactly, max|err| = 0.0e+00

   => a scalar descriptor block adds ZERO INFORMATION to fc1_gcn/fc1_gat.
      It can only change the OPTIMISATION (a better-conditioned, lower-
      dimensional parameterisation), never the hypothesis class.
      This is the same conclusion FINDINGS 8.1 reached for W6 by a different
      route (ProtT5 decodes identity at accuracy 1.000).

